[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own table says otherwise: the
reports merged into it have two causes, and a few of them are not a flip at all. This page splits
#4471 into the problems engineering has to fix, one ticket each.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes one of these problems. The other two stay open after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

A day-first tenant writes `07/04` for 7 April, and the parser reads 4 July. These reports come in
by app, by email and through booking-v2's form. This is the fix that is queued.

### `hint`: booking-v3's form tells people to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the tenant's way, day
first. People type what the hint says, and day and month swap. Only reports sent through the
booking-v3 form, at booking-v3 tenants, have this cause. The parser fix does not touch it: the form
has to print the tenant's own order.

### `one-day-move`: dates land a day early or late

Not a flip. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got
Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." The date moves by one day, which points at time zones, not
at day and month order. The desk read these in the report bodies (`desk-notes.json`). By tenant
they were filed under `parser`, so the parser fix would have closed them unfixed.

## How the reports are counted

- One report per id. The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant's locale and booking form, not from intake's tag. Intake guesses
  from the reporter's browser, and the guess is often wrong: compare "tagged as" below.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move counts there, wherever its tenant would file it.

## What #4471 shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A page can say one thing while its own table says another. Read the table.
- Merging on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put three problems in
  one ticket. A subject names the symptom, and all three share it: the wrong day.

